---

<LongForm
  title="About myleet"
  pageTitle="About"
  description="How myleet picks what you practice and when problems come back. Free and open source"
  toc={[
    ["what", "What myleet is"],
    ["ladder", "Review levels"],
    ["curriculum", "The curriculum"],
    ["companies", "Company sets"],
    ["free", "Free and open source"],
  ]}
>
  <h2 id="what">What myleet is</h2>
  <p>myleet is a practice app for software engineering internship, co-op and new-grad coding interviews. You read a short note on a pattern, solve problems in the browser against real Python tests, and myleet decides which problems come back and when.</p>
  <p>Your daily practice comes from your history: reviews that are due, techniques you keep missing, new problems in the order the notes teach them, and the companies in your goals. Everything runs in your browser with nothing to install, and a free account keeps your plan and history.</p>

  <h2 id="ladder">Review levels</h2>
  <p>Every problem you finish starts on level 1, and each level waits longer before bringing it back: 1 day on level 1, then 3, 7, 14 and 30 days on levels 2 to 5. When it comes back, a clean solve moves it up a level.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>To level up, pass the tests without a hint or a look at the solution, inside the 25-minute target.</li>
    <li>Using a hint, looking at the solution, going over time, or giving up sends it back to level 1. The attempt stays in your history.</li>
    <li>A second pass on the same day leaves the level where it is. It only moves up on a later day.</li>
    <li>A problem counts as mastered when it reaches level 4, which takes a clean pass a week after its level 3 review. It keeps coming back after that, 14 days later and then every 30 days.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="curriculum">The curriculum</h2>
  <p>The eighteen modules cover interview patterns such as arrays and hashing, two pointers, sliding window, binary search, trees, graphs and dynamic programming. The notes are meant to be read in order. Each one starts from the brute-force approach to a technique, then hands you problems to practice it on. Begin with the five <a href={ROUTES.learn}>start-here notes</a> on Learn.</p>
  <p>Problem statements belong to their original sources. myleet links to each one and shows it for practice without claiming it as its own.</p>

  <h2 id="companies">Company sets</h2>
  <p>Each company set holds practice problems for the techniques that come up most in that company's interviews, with the most common first. The sets are practice recommendations. They are not actual or leaked interview questions.</p>

  <h2 id="free">Free and open source</h2>
  <p>myleet is free, has no ads and is open source under the MIT license. The code, the curriculum and the trainer live at <a href="https://github.com/kidskoding/myleet" rel="noopener">github.com/kidskoding/myleet</a>. The same trainer runs as a command-line app and in the browser, so your history file works in both.</p>
  <p>Colors are <a href="https://catppuccin.com" rel="noopener">Catppuccin {FLAVOR_NAMES[SITE_FLAVOR]}</a>, MIT licensed. License notices for the open-source code that ships with myleet are in <a href="/licenses.txt">licenses.txt</a>.</p>
  <p>The <a href={ROUTES.privacy}>privacy page</a> lists what myleet stores, and the <a href={ROUTES.terms}>terms</a> cover the rules of use.</p>
</LongForm>
